	caption="A Blade Runner release added by hand, with its size, language, indexer, and flag."
	border />

<Callout type="tip">

Search simulations double as documentation. Databases can publish them and users can see how its profiles rank real releases!

</Callout>

## Simulating a Search

Start by choosing the profile you want to test from the profile menu (<InlineIcon icon="sliders" label="sliders icon" />). When you expand a movie or series, Profilarr scores each of its releases in three steps:

1. It runs the release title through the parser.
2. It checks the parsed release against every custom format in the database.
3. Each matched format is then added together to give a final score.

Here are the same four Blade Runner releases scored against two profiles:

<Screenshots items={profileScores} />

<Callout type="info">

A custom format is often scored by more than one profile, so changing it affects all of them. Since your test releases aren't tied to a profile, you can make the change once, then switch between the profiles that use it and check how each one ranks the same releases.

</Callout>

### Quick Parse

Press **Quick Parse** (<InlineIcon icon="flask" label="flask icon" />) to score a single title without saving it. Paste the title, choose whether it's a movie or a series, and choose a profile. Profilarr shows the same breakdown as an expanded release. Use it to check a title you've just seen in Radarr or Sonarr before deciding whether it's worth keeping as a test.
